<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Skye Boat Song</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">published <a href="/years/1884">1884</a>; the Gaelic song c. 1782</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;The Skye Boat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; (Roud 3772) is a late 19th-century Scottish song made from a Gaelic song composed around 1782 by William Ross, Cuachag nan Craobh (&quot;The Cuckoo in the Grove&quot;), in which the poet laments to a cuckoo that Lady Marion Ross, whom he loves, is rejecting him.</p></div>
